  "worked_solution": "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼1\\} \\\\  & =\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[2] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼2\\} \\\\  & =\\{2\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[3] & =\\{a\\in A∣a∼3\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,4\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe  skip $[4]$ since we already know that $4\\in [3]$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $3$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[1]=\\{1\\},\\,[2]=\\{2\\},\\,[3]=\\{3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected.",
